      "explanation": "This scenario presents a common and challenging ethical dilemma in end-of-life care, where there is a conflict between the medical team's assessment of futility and the family's desire for aggressive treatment. The most appropriate and ethical approach is to engage in open and compassionate communication. The doctor should convene a meeting with the family members (A), including the daughter, to discuss the patient's condition, the prognosis, and the goals of care. The focus should be on explaining why aggressive treatments are considered futile (i.e., they will not change the outcome and will likely only increase suffering) and reframing the goals from curative to palliative. The patient's own wishes and needs for comfort and dignity should be the central consideration. Simply acceding to the family's demands for futile treatment (B) is not ethically sound. Discharging the patient abruptly (C) or being deceptive with placebo care (D) are also inappropriate. A collaborative, empathetic discussion is the best course of action.",
