      "explanation": "An episiotomy is a surgical incision of the perineum and posterior vaginal wall. The repair is a layered closure performed in a specific sequence to ensure proper anatomical reapproximation and healing. The repair begins from the deepest layer and works outwards to the most superficial layer.\n1. Vaginal Mucosa : The first step is to identify the apex of the vaginal incision. A continuous locking or non-locking suture is used to close the vaginal mucosa, starting from the apex and proceeding down to the level of the hymenal ring.\n2. Perineal Muscles : After the vaginal mucosa is closed, the deeper perineal muscles that were incised (typically the bulbospongiosus and superficial transverse perineal muscles) are reapproximated with a few interrupted sutures. This step is crucial for restoring the structure and function of the perineal body.\n3. Skin : Finally, the perineal skin is closed. This can be done using a continuous subcuticular suture to achieve a better cosmetic result with less discomfort, or with interrupted sutures.\nThis \"inside-out\" sequence (Mucosa → Muscle → Skin) is the standard and correct technique.",
