      "correct": "A",
      "answerText": "(A) Controlled cord traction",
      "explanation": "The images clearly depict the technique of Controlled Cord Traction (CCT), which is a core component of the Active Management of the Third Stage of Labor (AMTSL). This maneuver is performed to facilitate the delivery of the placenta after it has separated from the uterine wall. The technique involves applying steady, gentle downward traction on the umbilical cord with one hand, while the other hand is placed on the lower abdomen just above the pubic symphysis to provide counter-pressure or \"guarding\" of the uterus. This counter-pressure stabilizes the uterus and prevents it from being pulled down and inverting (uterine inversion), a rare but life-threatening complication. CCT should only be applied after a uterotonic drug has been given and signs of placental separation are evident. This is different from manual removal of the placenta (C), which involves inserting a hand into the uterus to detach the placenta.",
