      "explanation": "An accurate estimation of the gestational age is a critical component of antenatal care. Ultrasound is the most reliable method for dating a pregnancy in the first trimester. The most accurate biometric parameter for determining the gestational age during the first trimester (up to about 13-14 weeks) is the crown-rump length (CRL). The CRL is the measurement of the length of the embryo or fetus from the top of its head (crown) to the bottom of its buttocks (rump). The CRL grows in a very predictable manner during early pregnancy, and its measurement has a very small margin of error (typically +/- 5 to 7 days). The mean gestational sac diameter (B) is used very early, before the embryo is visible. The biparietal diameter (A) and abdominal circumference (C) are parameters used for dating and assessing growth in the second and third trimesters, but they are less accurate than the first-trimester CRL.",
