      "explanation": "A Non-Stress Test (NST) is a common antenatal test used to assess fetal well-being. A \"reactive\" NST, which is reassuring, is defined by the presence of two or more fetal heart rate accelerations (of at least 15 beats per minute, lasting at least 15 seconds) within a 20-minute period. In this case, after 20 minutes, only one acceleration has been observed. This is classified as a \"nonreactive\" NST. However, a nonreactive NST does not immediately signify fetal distress. The most common reason for a nonreactive NST is that the fetus is in a quiet sleep cycle. Therefore, the standard protocol is to continue the NST for another 20 minutes (for a total of 40 minutes) to allow for the fetus to wake up and become more active. If the test remains nonreactive after 40 minutes, further evaluation with a biophysical profile or contraction stress test is warranted. Immediate delivery (A, B) is not indicated based on an initial 20-minute nonreactive NST.",
