      "explanation": "Binocular single vision is a graded process with three levels.\n1. Grade I : Simultaneous Perception. This is the most basic level, where the brain is able to perceive two separate images, one from each eye, at the same time. Image A illustrates this : the right eye sees a cage, the left eye sees a bird, and the brain perceives both simultaneously, often superimposing them.\n2. Grade II : Fusion. This is the ability of the brain to take the two slightly dissimilar images from each eye and combine, or fuse, them into a single, unified image. Image B illustrates this : the right eye and left eye each see a squirrel with a part of it missing. The brain fuses these two incomplete images to perceive a single, complete squirrel.\n3. Grade III : Stereopsis. This is the highest level of binocular vision, where the brain uses the slight horizontal disparity between the two images to perceive depth and three-dimensionality. The image does not explicitly depict stereopsis.",
