      "answerText": "(B) Apoptosis and Pyroptosis",
      "explanation": "Caspases are a family of cysteine proteases that are the central executioners of programmed cell death.\n1. Apoptosis is the classic form of programmed cell death and is fundamentally a caspase-dependent process. It can be initiated through the intrinsic (mitochondrial) pathway, which converges on the activation of initiator caspase-9, or the extrinsic (death receptor) pathway, which activates initiator caspase-8. Both pathways lead to the activation of executioner caspases like caspase-3, which dismantle the cell.\n2. Pyroptosis is a highly inflammatory form of programmed cell death that is also caspase-dependent. It is typically triggered by microbial pathogens and involves the activation of an inflammasome, which leads to the activation of caspase-1 (or caspases-4/5/11). Active caspase-1 cleaves pro-inflammatory cytokines (IL-1β, IL-18) and also a protein called gasdermin D, which forms pores in the cell membrane, leading to cell swelling and lytic death.\nNecrosis (A) is an unregulated form of cell death, and necroptosis (C) is a programmed form of necrosis that is specifically caspase-independent.",
