      "correct": "C",
      "answerText": "(C) Flow cytometry",
      "explanation": "Flow cytometry is the gold-standard laboratory technique for the analysis of cell surface and intracellular antigens, known as Cluster of Differentiation (CD) markers. This powerful technology allows for the rapid, multiparametric analysis of single cells in a suspension. Cells are labeled with fluorescently-tagged monoclonal antibodies that are specific for different CD markers. As the cells flow one-by-one through a laser beam, the fluorochromes are excited and emit light, which is detected and quantified. This allows for the precise quantification of cell populations based on their unique CD marker expression profiles (immunophenotyping). It is an indispensable tool in hematology and immunology for diagnosing and classifying leukemias and lymphomas, monitoring HIV infection (by counting CD4 T-cells), and assessing immune status. Immunohistochemistry (IHC) (D) also detects these markers but is qualitative and performed on tissue sections, not for quantification in a cell suspension.",
