      "explanation": "The description provided is the classic profile of Follicular Lymphoma. This is a common type of indolent B-cell non-Hodgkin lymphoma. Histologically, it is characterized by a nodular (follicular) growth pattern composed of a mixture of small, cleaved cells (centrocytes) and larger, non-cleaved cells (centroblasts), which recapitulate a normal germinal center. The immuno phenotype is that of a germinal center B-cell, with positivity for CD20, CD10, and BCL6. The defining molecular hallmark of over 85% of follicular lymphomas is the specific chromosomal translocation t(14;18)(q32;q21). This translocation juxtaposes the BCL2 gene on chromosome 18 with the immunoglobulin heavy chain gene locus on chromosome 14. This leads to the massive overexpression of the BCL2 protein, which is an anti-apoptotic protein. This overexpression prevents the normal process of apoptosis, leading to the accumulation of the neoplastic B-cells.",
