      "explanation": "The cortical reaction is the biological mechanism that ensures only one sperm fertilizes the ovum and prevents the entry of additional sperms, a condition known as polyspermy. After a sperm successfully penetrates the plasma membrane of the ovum, the egg immediately triggers this protective reaction.\nDuring the cortical reaction, small membrane-bound vesicles called cortical granules, which lie just beneath the ovum’s plasma membrane, release their contents into the space between the plasma membrane and the zona pellucida. These released enzymes cause chemical modifications in the zona pellucida, leading to its hardening and structural alteration.\nAs a result, the zona pellucida becomes impermeable to other sperms. This modification prevents additional sperms from binding to or penetrating the egg surface. Thus, the cortical reaction acts as a fast and effective block against polyspermy, ensuring that the zygote receives the correct diploid number of chromosomes.\nOther options represent different processes in fertilization. The acrosome reaction helps sperm penetrate the egg coverings, capacitation prepares sperm for fertilization in the female reproductive tract, and the zona reaction is a secondary modification of the zona pellucida following the cortical reaction. Therefore, the cortical reaction is the primary mechanism preventing multiple sperm entry.",
