      "number": 58,
      "statement": "What type of enzyme inhibition is demonstrated by substance A?",
      "options": [
        "Competitive inhibition",
        "Uncompetitive inhibition",
        "Mixed inhibition",
        "Non competitive inhibition"
      ],
      "correct": "A",
      "answerText": "(A) Competitive inhibition",
      "explanation": "The graph shown is a Lineweaver–Burk plot (double reciprocal plot), where the y-axis represents (1/V_0) and the x-axis represents (1/[S]). This plot is commonly used in enzyme kinetics to analyze different types of enzyme inhibition.\nIn the figure, the line representing the reaction with inhibitor (A) intersects the y-axis at the same point as the line without inhibitor, but the slope of the inhibited line is steeper. This indicates that the maximum velocity (Vmax) remains unchanged, while the apparent Km increases.\nThis pattern is characteristic of competitive inhibition. In competitive inhibition, the inhibitor resembles the substrate and competes with it for binding at the enzyme’s active site. Because the inhibitor and substrate compete for the same site, increasing the substrate concentration can overcome the inhibition.\nOn a Lineweaver–Burk plot, competitive inhibition produces lines that intersect on the y-axis, since Vmax stays constant but Km increases, resulting in a steeper slope.\nOther types of inhibition show different patterns:\nUncompetitive inhibition produces parallel lines, while non-competitive inhibition changes Vmax without affecting Km.\nTherefore, the inhibition shown in the graph is competitive inhibition.",
