      "explanation": "In the setting of acute traumatic brain injury with cerebral edema and signs of increased intracranial pressure (ICP), the diuretic of choice is an osmotic diuretic, with mannitol being the gold standard. Mannitol is a large sugar molecule that is administered intravenously. It remains in the intravascular space, increasing the osmotic pressure of the blood. This creates an osmotic gradient that draws excess water out of the brain tissue (the edematous parenchyma) and into the bloodstream, thereby reducing cerebral edema and lowering ICP. The excess fluid is then excreted by the kidneys. Hypertonic saline is another osmotic agent used for this purpose. Loop diuretics like furosemide (Option C) are less effective for cerebral edema and are sometimes used as adjuncts. Spironolactone (Option B) and hydrochlorothiazide (Option D) have no role in the acute management of cerebral edema.",
