      "correct": "A",
      "answerText": "(A) Celiac disease",
      "explanation": "Celiac disease is an autoimmune disorder triggered by ingestion of gluten, a protein found in wheat, barley, and rye. It leads to immune-mediated damage of the small intestinal mucosa, particularly the villi, resulting in malabsorption.\nIn children, common symptoms include chronic diarrhea, abdominal pain, bloating, weight loss, and nutritional deficiencies. A distinctive extraintestinal manifestation is pruritic skin lesions, known as dermatitis herpetiformis, which occur due to IgA deposition in the dermal papillae. This skin condition is strongly associated with celiac disease and improves with a gluten-free diet.\nBecause gluten triggers the immune response that damages the intestinal lining, the primary treatment is lifelong avoidance of gluten-containing foods. Following a gluten-free diet helps restore the intestinal villi and reduces both gastrointestinal and skin symptoms.\nThe other options are less consistent with the scenario. Whipple's disease is a rare bacterial infection, Crohn’s disease is an inflammatory bowel disease not specifically related to gluten, and dermatitis herpetiformis is actually a cutaneous manifestation of celiac disease rather than the underlying cause.\nTherefore, the most likely underlying etiology is celiac disease.",
