      "explanation": "A Carhart’s notch is a characteristic finding on pure tone audiometry, typically seen in otosclerosis. It appears as a dip in bone conduction thresholds around 2000 Hz, caused by fixation of the stapes footplate at the oval window. This fixation disrupts normal sound transmission through the ossicular chain.\nBecause otosclerosis primarily affects the middle ear conduction mechanism, the hearing loss is usually conductive in nature. In conductive hearing loss, the Rinne test becomes negative, meaning bone conduction is better than air conduction (BC > AC) in the affected ear.\nOther findings may also be associated with otosclerosis, but they are less directly linked to Carhart’s notch. Schwartz sign is a reddish blush seen on the tympanic membrane due to vascularization of the promontory in active otosclerosis. Hitselberger sign indicates facial nerve involvement and is associated with acoustic neuroma. Hennebert sign refers to vertigo induced by pressure changes and is seen in syphilitic labyrinthitis.\nSince Carhart’s notch indicates otosclerosis with conductive hearing loss, the most likely associated clinical test finding is a negative Rinne test.",
