      "explanation": "The most common cause of a gradual, painless decrease in vision months to years after successful cataract surgery is Posterior Capsular Opacification (PCO), sometimes called a secondary cataract. During modern cataract surgery, the cloudy natural lens is removed, but the thin, clear posterior membrane of the lens capsule is left intact to support the new intraocular lens (IOL). Over time, residual lens epithelial cells on the capsule can proliferate and migrate across the posterior capsule, causing it to become hazy, wrinkled, and opacified, as seen in the image. This blocks light from reaching the retina, causing blurred vision and glare. PCO is easily and effectively treated with a non-invasive laser procedure called a YAG laser capsulotomy, which creates a clear opening in the center of the cloudy capsule.",
