      "explanation": "The patient presents with altered consciousness and severe hypoglycemia (glucose 45 mg/dL) in the context of chronic alcoholism. This is a medical emergency. The critical management principle in this situation is to correct the hypoglycemia without precipitating Wernicke's encephalopathy. Chronic alcoholics are frequently deficient in thiamine (vitamin B1), a vital coenzyme for glucose metabolism. If a large load of glucose (dextrose) is given before thiamine, it can deplete the remaining thiamine stores and cause acute, irreversible brain damage. Therefore, the standard and mandatory protocol is to administer thiamine first (intramuscularly or intravenously) to ensure the metabolic pathways are functional, followed immediately by intravenous dextrose to correct the life-threatening hypoglycemia. Administering only dextrose (Option D) is dangerous. Normal saline (Option A) will not correct the hypoglycemia.",
