      "explanation": "The patient's signs and symptoms— hypertension, tachycardia (rapid heart rate), mydriasis (dilated pupils), and hyperthermia (elevated body temperature)—constitute a classic sympathomimetic toxidrome. This is caused by substances that stimulate the sympathetic nervous system. Cocaine is a potent sympathomimetic that blocks the reuptake of norepinephrine, dopamine, and serotonin, leading to excessive stimulation of adrenergic receptors. This results in the cardiovascular stimulation (tachycardia, hypertension), pupillary dilation, and increased metabolic rate (hyperthermia) seen in the patient. Heroin (Option A) and morphine (Option B) are opioids and would cause the opposite effects : CNS depression, bradycardia, hypotension, and miosis (constricted pupils). Chlorpheniramine (Option D) is an antihistamine that in overdose would cause an anticholinergic toxidrome (tachycardia, mydriasis, but with dry skin, not typically diaphoresis).",
