      "correct": "C",
      "answerText": "(C) Chance fracture",
      "explanation": "The fracture pattern described—a horizontal fracture line extending through the vertebral body, pedicles, and spinous process—is the definition of a Chance fracture. This is an unstable flexion-distraction type injury of the spine. It is classically caused by a rapid deceleration mechanism where the upper body is thrown forward while the lower body is restrained, such as by a lap seatbelt in a motor vehicle accident (hence the alternate name seatbelt fracture). The vertebra is essentially pulled apart horizontally. A burst fracture (Option A) is a compression injury where the vertebra is crushed, with retropulsion of bone fragments into the spinal canal. A simple compression fracture (Option B) involves collapse of the anterior part of the vertebral body. Spondylolisthesis (Option D) is the slippage of one vertebra over another. The purely horizontal, distraction nature of the fracture is key to the diagnosis.",
