      "explanation": "Posterior Urethral Valves (PUV) are the most common cause of congenital bladder outlet obstruction in males. The diagnosis is confirmed by a VCUG showing a dilated and elongated posterior urethra, a thickened, trabeculated bladder, and often vesicoureteral reflux. The obstruction must be relieved to prevent progressive, irreversible damage to the bladder and kidneys. The definitive and standard treatment for PUV is primary endoscopic valve ablation. This procedure is performed using a pediatric cystoscope or resectoscope passed through the urethra. The obstructing valve leaflets are identified and incised or fulgurated (ablated), thereby relieving the obstruction. While a temporary urinary diversion like a suprapubic cystostomy (Option D) might be needed in a very sick neonate for initial stabilization, the primary corrective surgery is valve ablation. Observation or just antibiotics (Options A, B) is inappropriate as the mechanical obstruction will persist.",
