      "explanation": "The combination of the patient's age, history of falls, and subacute/chronic onset of neurological symptoms is classic for a chronic subdural hematoma (SDH). The CT finding confirms this diagnosis. A crescent-shaped (concave inwards) collection of fluid that layers over the cerebral hemisphere and crosses suture lines is characteristic of a subdural hematoma. The fact that the collection is hypodense (darker than brain tissue) indicates that it is chronic; the blood has been there for several weeks and has broken down into serous fluid. An acute SDH (Option A) would be hyperdense (bright white). An epidural hematoma (Option B) is typically biconvex (lens-shaped) and is usually acute and hyperdense. An intracerebral hemorrhage (Option D) would be located within the brain parenchyma. The gradual onset of symptoms is due to the slow expansion of the hematoma, often from minor head trauma causing tearing of bridging veins.",
