      "number": 264,
      "statement": "A 40-year-old man presents with headaches, fever, and new-onset seizures. Brain MRI shows a ring-enhancing lesion with central restricted diffusion on DWI. What is the most likely diagnosis?",
      "options": [
        "Glioblastoma multiforme",
        "Metastatic brain tumor",
        "Brain abscess",
        "Toxoplasmosis"
      ],
      "correct": "C",
      "answerText": "(C) Brain abscess",
      "explanation": "The clinical triad of headache, fever, and focal neurological deficits (like seizures) is highly suggestive of a central nervous system infection. On MRI, while several pathologies can cause a ring-enhancing lesion (tumors, infections), the finding of central restricted diffusion on Diffusion-Weighted Imaging (DWI) is a key diagnostic clue. This feature, which appears bright on DWI maps and dark on ADC maps, is highly specific for a pyogenic brain abscess. The restricted diffusion is caused by the high viscosity and cellularity of the purulent material (pus) within the abscess cavity, which restricts the random Brownian motion of water molecules. In contrast, the necrotic core of a glioblastoma (Option A) or a metastatic tumor (Option B) is typically not diffusion-restricted (it shows facilitated diffusion). While toxoplasmosis (Option D) can show ring enhancement, it usually does not have central restricted diffusion.",
      "pages": [
        119,
        196
      ],
      "difficulty": "Medium",
      "difficultyEstimated": true,
      "topic": "Radiology"
