      "answerText": "(A) Cormack and Lehane",
      "explanation": "The image shows a laryngoscopic view of the larynx, commonly seen during endotracheal intubation. The Cormack–Lehane classification is used to grade the view of the glottis during direct laryngoscopy and helps assess the difficulty of intubation. Cormack–Lehane Grading System\n1. Grade I: Full view of the glottis\n2. Grade II: Only partial view of the glottis\n3. Grade III: Only the epiglottis is visible, no glottic opening\n4. Grade IV:Neither the glottis nor epiglottis is visible This classification is particularly relevant in patients with a history of intubation or airway difficulty, as it helps anesthesiologists anticipate airway management challenges. Why Other Options Are Incorrect\n1. AJCC – Used for cancer staging.\n2. TNM – Tumor staging system for malignancies.\n3. Radkowski classification – Used for juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma staging. Thus, the staging system used for the condition shown in the image is the Cormack–Lehane classification.",
