      "number": 268,
      "statement": "What is the name of the nerve block technique demonstrated in the image?",
      "options": [
        "Intra-arterial anesthesia",
        "Bier's block",
        "Regional anesthesia",
        "Axillary block"
      ],
      "correct": "B",
      "answerText": "(B) Bier's block",
      "explanation": "The technique depicted in the image is Intravenous Regional Anesthesia (IVRA), more commonly known as a Bier's block. This procedure involves placing a double-cuffed tourniquet on the proximal part of the limb (e.g., the upper arm). The limb is then exsanguinated (drained of blood) by elevating it and wrapping it tightly with an Esmarch bandage. The proximal cuff of the tourniquet is inflated to above systolic pressure to create a bloodless field. The Esmarch bandage is removed, and a large volume of dilute local anesthetic (typically lidocaine) is injected intravenously into the distal part of the limb. The tourniquet prevents the anesthetic from entering the systemic circulation, allowing it to diffuse into the tissues of the limb and produce dense anesthesia and muscle relaxation. This technique is used for short surgical procedures on the hand, forearm, or foot. Regional anesthesia (C) is a general term. An axillary block (D) involves injecting anesthetic around the brachial plexus in the axilla.",
