      "explanation": "The symptoms of muscle rigidity, tremors, and trismus (lockjaw) after administration of an intravenous anesthetic are characteristic of myoclonus, which is a known side effect of etomidate. Etomidate is an IV anesthetic agent valued for its hemodynamic stability (minimal effect on blood pressure and heart rate), making it useful for induction in critically ill or trauma patients. However, its use is associated with a high incidence of excitatory phenomena, including involuntary muscle movements (myoclonus) and pain on injection. The myoclonus is thought to be caused by disinhibition of subcortical neuronal activity. These movements can be minimized by pre-treatment with opioids or benzodiazepines. Thiopentone (C) is a barbiturate, and halothane (A) is an inhaled agent; neither is typically associated with myoclonus. Nitrous oxide (B) can cause shivering but not rigidity and trismus.",
