      "answerText": "(A) Tuberculosis",
      "explanation": "The laboratory values provided are used in Light's criteria to differentiate between an exudative and a transudative pleural effusion. A pleural effusion is classified as exudative if it meets at least one of the following : 1) Pleural fluid protein / serum protein ratio > 0.5; 2) Pleural fluid LDH / serum LDH ratio > 0.6; or 3) Pleural fluid LDH > two-thirds of the upper limit of normal for serum LDH. This patient's fluid meets the first two criteria, confirming it is an exudate. Exudative effusions are caused by inflammation or disease of the pleural surface, leading to increased capillary permeability. The most common causes are malignancy, pneumonia, and tuberculosis. In contrast, heart failure (B), cirrhosis (hepatic failure, C), and nephrotic syndrome (renal failure, D) are classic causes of transudative effusions, which result from imbalances in hydrostatic or oncotic pressures.",
