      "explanation": "Millard–Gubler syndrome is a ventral pontine syndrome caused by a lesion in the anterior part of the pons, most commonly due to a basilar artery branch infarction. The syndrome characteristically produces a pattern of “crossed paralysis,” where cranial nerve deficits occur on one side of the face while motor weakness occurs on the opposite side of the body.\nIn this patient, right-sided facial paralysis indicates involvement of the facial nerve (cranial nerve VII) in the pons. The lateral rectus palsy suggests damage to the abducens nerve (cranial nerve VI), which controls abduction of the eye. Both of these cranial nerves originate in the pontine region, confirming that the lesion is located in the pons.\nAt the same time, weakness of the left arm and leg indicates involvement of the corticospinal tract, which carries motor signals from the brain to the body. Since corticospinal fibers cross in the lower medulla, a pontine lesion produces contralateral limb weakness.\nThus, the combination of ipsilateral VI and VII cranial nerve palsy with contralateral hemiparesis is the classic clinical feature of Millard–Gubler syndrome.",
