      "answerText": "(C) Autosomal Dominant Polycystic Kidney Disease (ADPKD)",
      "explanation": "The clinical triad of hypertension, hematuria, and flank pain, along with the MRI finding of bilaterally enlarged kidneys containing innumerable cysts of varying sizes, is the classic presentation of Autosomal Dominant Polycystic Kidney Disease (ADPKD). This is a common genetic disorder where the progressive growth of cysts throughout the renal parenchyma leads to the destruction of normal kidney tissue, massive kidney enlargement, and eventual renal failure. The cysts can also cause pain by stretching the renal capsule or by bleeding into a cyst. Hypertension develops early and is a major complication. While acquired cystic disease can occur in chronic kidney disease (D), the massive enlargement and extensive cystic replacement are characteristic of ADPKD. A simple renal cyst (A) would be a solitary finding, and a tumor (B) would have a different appearance.",
