      "explanation": "TTF-1, which stands for Thyroid Transcription Factor-1, is a protein that plays a vital role in the normal development of the lung and thyroid gland. Because of this specific function, it serves as an excellent lineage marker in cancer pathology. Tumors that arise from lung or thyroid tissue often continue to express this protein, which helps pathologists confidently identify a tumor's origin.\nIn the context of lung cancer, TTF-1 is invaluable. It is positive in approximately 75-85% of lung adenocarcinomas and 85-95% of small cell carcinomas. Its most critical clinical application is in diagnosing metastatic cancer of unknown primary origin. For example, if a patient has a tumor in the brain or liver, a positive TTF-1 stain on the biopsy strongly points to a primary lung cancer, which is essential for determining the correct chemotherapy regimen.\nTTF-1 is also positive in most thyroid cancers. In contrast, (C) Thymoma and (D) Melanoma arise from different cell lineages and are consistently negative for TTF-1, relying on other specific markers for their diagnosis.",
