      "answerText": "(A) Testicular torsion with gangrene",
      "explanation": "Testicular torsion is a surgical emergency that commonly occurs in adolescent boys. It results from twisting of the spermatic cord, which obstructs blood flow to the testis. If the torsion is not corrected promptly, the testicular tissue becomes ischemic and may progress to gangrene.\nIn this case, the patient has acute scrotal pain following trauma with a delay of about 6 hours before surgical exploration. Testicular torsion can sometimes be triggered by trauma or sudden movement. When blood supply remains compromised for several hours, the testis becomes dark, swollen, and necrotic, indicating gangrenous changes. The salvage rate of the testis is highest within 4–6 hours of torsion. Beyond this time, the risk of irreversible ischemic damage increases significantly.\nTorsion of the testicular appendage typically causes milder pain and may show a “blue dot sign.” A hematoma would present as localized bleeding without twisting of the spermatic cord.\nTherefore, the surgical finding most consistent with severe ischemic damage after torsion is testicular torsion with gangrene.",
