      "explanation": "The image shows a pressure dressing used in negative pressure wound therapy (NPWT), commonly known as vacuum-assisted closure. This technique applies controlled suction to a wound using a sealed dressing connected to a suction device.\nNegative pressure wound therapy is widely used for chronic wounds, pressure ulcers, traumatic wounds, diabetic ulcers, and post-surgical wounds. The therapy works by removing excess fluid, reducing edema, improving blood flow, and promoting the formation of granulation tissue, which accelerates wound healing.\nThe ideal negative pressure maintained in most NPWT systems is about 125 mm Hg, usually applied either continuously or intermittently. Research has shown that this level of pressure optimizes tissue perfusion and granulation tissue formation while effectively removing wound exudate. Lower pressures such as 60–80 mm Hg or 80–100 mm Hg may sometimes be used in sensitive patients, but they are generally less effective for standard therapy. Pressures around 125 mm Hg are considered the standard recommended setting for most wounds. Therefore, the ideal pressure to be maintained with this dressing is approximately 125 mm Hg.",
