      "explanation": "After surgical resection of an oral malignancy, adjuvant (postoperative) radiotherapy is given to reduce the risk of locoregional recurrence. The decision is based on pathological risk factors. The single most important and highest-risk feature indicating a need for postoperative radiation (often with concurrent chemotherapy) is the presence of extranodal extension (ENE), also called extracapsular spread. ENE refers to the finding of cancer cells that have breached the capsule of a lymph node and invaded into the surrounding soft tissues of the neck. This signifies a much more aggressive disease and is associated with a significantly worse prognosis and a high risk of recurrence. While multiple positive nodes (Option B), a large primary tumor (T3, Option C), and close surgical margins (Option D) are also considered adverse features and may be indications for radiotherapy, ENE is considered the strongest indication.",
