      "explanation": "The patient has a slowly enlarging, non-healing ulcerative lesion on the lower lip, which raises strong suspicion for Squamous cell carcinoma. This type of cancer commonly occurs on the lower lip, especially in elderly individuals with chronic sun exposure or tobacco use.\nFor suspected oral malignancies, the most appropriate biopsy technique is to take a superficial incisional biopsy from the edge of the lesion where abnormal tissue meets normal mucosa. This area contains viable tumor cells and the transition zone, which helps the pathologist accurately identify malignant changes.\nBiopsying the central ulcerated area alone is not ideal, because it may contain necrotic or inflamed tissue, which can lead to an inconclusive diagnosis.\nExcisional biopsy is usually avoided initially in large suspicious lesions because complete removal without proper margins may complicate future definitive surgery.\nA deep tissue biopsy alone is unnecessary for initial diagnosis.\nTherefore, the best method to establish a definitive diagnosis in this case is a superficial incisional biopsy taken from the border of the lesion including adjacent normal tissue.",
