      "correct": "B",
      "answerText": "(B) Inosculation",
      "explanation": "The survival of a skin graft depends on it acquiring a new blood supply from the underlying wound bed. This process occurs in three phases :\n1. Imbibition (Days 1-2) : In the first 24-48 hours, the graft survives by absorbing nutrients and oxygen from the serous fluid that collects between the graft and the wound bed. The graft becomes edematous and gains weight during this phase.\n2. Inosculation (Days 3-5) : Around day 3, a crucial event called inosculation occurs. The existing blood vessels in the dermis of the skin graft make direct, end-to-end connections (anastomoses) with the budding capillaries from the recipient wound bed. This establishes an initial, primitive circulation.\n3. Neovascularization (After Day 5) : Following inosculation, new blood vessels grow from the wound bed into the graft, establishing a more robust and permanent circulation.\nTherefore, on day 3, the primary mechanism of revascularization and nutrition is inosculation.",
