      "explanation": "The clinical presentation of fasting hypoglycemia (fatigue and hunger between meals) that is relieved by feeding, combined with the definitive finding of an absence of glycogen in the liver, points to a defect in glycogen synthesis. The key enzyme responsible for creating glycogen from glucose is glycogen synthase. A deficiency in this enzyme (Glycogen Storage Disease Type 0) means the liver cannot store glucose as glycogen after a meal. As a result, during a fast, the liver has no glycogen stores to break down (glycogenolysis) to maintain blood glucose levels, leading to hypoglycemia. Deficiencies in branching (A) or debranching (B) enzymes lead to abnormal glycogen structures, not absence. Glucose-6-phosphatase deficiency (C) leads to a massive accumulation of normal glycogen.",
