      "explanation": "The patients symptoms are classic for Carpal Tunnel Syndrome (CTS), which is a compression neuropathy of the median nerve at the wrist. Key features include nocturnal paresthesias (tingling and numbness) in the median nerve distribution (thumb, index, middle, and radial half of the ring finger) and relief of symptoms by shaking or hanging the hand down (the flick sign). The Durkan test, or carpal compression test, is a provocative maneuver used to diagnose CTS. It involves the examiner applying firm, direct pressure over the carpal tunnel for up to 30 seconds. The test is positive if it reproduces the patients symptoms of tingling or numbness in the median nerve distribution. Froments sign (A, C) is a test for ulnar nerve palsy. Guyon canal syndrome involves the ulnar nerve at the wrist.",
