      "explanation": "The green shaded region in the diagram represents the mid pelvis (mid-pelvic cavity). The pelvis is anatomically divided into three important obstetric levels: the pelvic inlet, mid pelvis, and pelvic outlet. These divisions are important for understanding the passage of the fetal head during childbirth.\nThe pelvic inlet is the upper opening of the true pelvis. It is bounded by the sacral promontory posteriorly, the arcuate line of the ilium laterally, and the pubic symphysis anteriorly. This region marks the entrance of the pelvic cavity.\nThe mid pelvis, which is shown in green in the figure, lies between the pelvic inlet and pelvic outlet. It corresponds to the level of the ischial spines and the middle part of the sacrum. This region is clinically important because the ischial spines are the narrowest transverse diameter of the pelvis and are used as landmarks to assess the station of the fetal head during labor.\nThe pelvic outlet is the inferior opening of the pelvis, bounded by the tip of the coccyx, ischial tuberosities, and pubic arch.\nSince the shaded region corresponds to the central cavity at the level of the ischial spines, it represents the mid pelvis.",
