      "explanation": "The image shows a firearm entry wound with surrounding blackening and tattooing, which is characteristic of an intermediate-range pistol shot.\nIn forensic medicine, firearm injuries are classified based on the distance between the muzzle and the target. In intermediate-range shots, the bullet is accompanied by unburnt or partially burnt gunpowder particles that strike the skin. These particles produce tattooing (stippling) around the wound. Tattooing appears as multiple small punctate abrasions that cannot be wiped off.\nThe lesion in the image shows a central circular entry wound with surrounding reddish-black punctate marks, indicating deposition of gunpowder particles, which is typical for intermediate-range firing.\nIn contrast, close or contact shots usually show burning, blackening (soot deposition), and sometimes muzzle imprint, while distant shots show only the entry wound without blackening or tattooing. Rifle shots typically occur from longer distances and may show different wound characteristics depending on the range.\nTherefore, the presence of tattooing around the entry wound indicates an intermediate-range pistol shot.",
