      "answerText": "(C) ALA Dehydratase",
      "explanation": "Lead poisoning (plumbism) disrupts the heme synthesis pathway by inhibiting several key enzymes, leading to anemia and the accumulation of precursors. Two of the most clinically significant enzymes inhibited by lead are :\n1. Aminolevulinic acid (ALA) dehydratase : This enzyme catalyzes an early step in the pathway. Its inhibition leads to an accumulation of ALA in the blood and urine, which is a sensitive marker of lead exposure.\n2. Ferrochelatase : This is the final enzyme in the pathway, responsible for inserting iron into the protoporphyrin IX ring to form heme. Its inhibition leads to an accumulation of protoporphyrin in red blood cells (erythrocyte protoporphyrin or zinc protoporphyrin).\nBoth enzymes are critically affected. Given that ALA dehydratase is an option and its inhibition is a hallmark of lead toxicity, it is a correct answer. Ferrochelatase is also correct.",
