      "correct": "A",
      "answerText": "(A) N-acetylcysteine",
      "explanation": "The patient has taken a toxic dose of paracetamol (acetaminophen). Paracetamol overdose is a major cause of acute liver failure. In the liver, a small fraction of paracetamol is metabolized by the cytochrome P450 system into a highly toxic intermediate called N-acetyl-p-benzoquinone imine (NAPQI). Normally, NAPQI is detoxified by conjugation with glutathione. In an overdose, the glutathione stores are depleted, and the excess NAPQI accumulates, causing severe hepatocellular necrosis. The specific antidote for paracetamol poisoning is N-acetylcysteine (NAC). NAC acts as a precursor for glutathione, thereby replenishing the liver's stores and enhancing the detoxification of NAPQI. It may also bind directly to NAPQI. It is most effective when administered within 8 hours of ingestion but can provide benefit even later. Naloxone (B) and naltrexone (C) are opioid antagonists.",
