      "explanation": "The burn area in the image involves approximately one side of the body (anterior half of one side) in a 10-year-old child. To estimate burn surface area in children, clinicians commonly use the Rule of Nines (with slight pediatric modifications) or the Lund and Browder chart, which provides a more accurate assessment for children.\nAccording to the Rule of Nines, the body surface is divided into sections representing percentages of the Total Body Surface Area (TBSA). In children, the approximate values are similar but slightly adjusted because the head accounts for a larger percentage and the lower limbs slightly less compared to adults.\nIn the image, the burn shading covers half of the anterior trunk and half of the anterior limbs on one side of the body. The anterior trunk contributes about 18% TBSA, so half of it equals about 9%. The involvement of parts of the upper limb and lower limb on the same side adds additional percentage, bringing the total to roughly 15–20% TBSA.\nThus, the estimated burn surface area is approximately 15–20%.",
