      "explanation": "Sympathetic ophthalmia is a rare, bilateral, diffuse granulomatous inflammation that occurs after a penetrating injury or intraocular surgery to one eye (the exciting eye). A few weeks to many years after the initial trauma, an autoimmune response is triggered against exposed retinal antigens (like melanin-associated proteins), leading to inflammation in both the injured eye and the previously healthy, uninjured sympathizing eye. The inflammation characteristically affects the entire uveal tract—the iris, ciliary body, and choroid. Inflammation of all parts of the uveal tract is known as panuveitis. Clinically, this presents with signs of both anterior uveitis (pain, photophobia, keratic precipitates) and posterior uveitis (vitritis, choroidal lesions like Dalen-Fuchs nodules, and optic disc edema). It is a severe, sight-threatening condition requiring aggressive immunosuppressive therapy.",
