      "explanation": "In cases of penetrating abdominal stab injury, management depends largely on the hemodynamic status of the patient and the extent of injury. In this scenario, examination reveals penetration of the fascia, which indicates that the wound has crossed the abdominal wall and may involve intra-abdominal organs. However, the patient’s vital signs are stable, meaning there is no immediate evidence of life-threatening hemorrhage or shock.\nFor hemodynamically stable patients with penetrating abdominal trauma, the next best step is further evaluation with imaging and diagnostic tests rather than immediate surgery. Investigations may include contrast-enhanced CT scan of the abdomen, Focused Assessment with Sonography for Trauma (FAST), or local wound exploration to determine whether the peritoneum has been breached and whether internal organs are injured.\nImmediate exploratory laparotomy is reserved for patients who are hemodynamically unstable, have signs of peritonitis, evisceration, or obvious internal injury. Simply waiting after dressing the wound could miss serious intra-abdominal injury, and IV fluids alone do not address the need for diagnosis.\nTherefore, in a stable patient with fascial penetration, the most appropriate next step is further investigation to assess intra-abdominal damage.",
