      "explanation": "McArdle disease is Glycogen Storage Disease Type V. It is a metabolic myopathy caused by a deficiency of the enzyme myophosphorylase, which is the muscle isoform of glycogen phosphorylase. This enzyme is responsible for the first step of glycogenolysis (the breakdown of glycogen) in skeletal muscle. In McArdle disease, the muscles are unable to break down their glycogen stores to produce glucose for energy. This leads to the classic symptoms of exercise intolerance, severe muscle fatigue, cramping, and pain that occur shortly after starting strenuous activity. Patients often experience a second wind phenomenon if they rest and allow blood-borne fuels like glucose and free fatty acids to reach the muscles. A failure of serum lactate to rise after ischemic forearm exercise is a characteristic diagnostic finding. Glucose-6-phosphatase (A) is deficient in Von Gierke disease. Lysosomal glucosidase (C) is deficient in Pompe disease.",
