      "explanation": "A Galeazzi fracture is defined as a fracture of the distal third of the radius associated with dislocation or disruption of the distal radioulnar joint (DRUJ). It is considered an unstable forearm injury, especially in adults.\nIn the emergency department, the first step in management is always to assess vascular status of the limb. This includes checking radial and ulnar pulses, capillary refill, skin temperature, and signs of distal ischemia, because trauma can compromise blood supply.\nAfter confirming vascular integrity, the next step is closed reduction of the fracture and the distal radioulnar joint to restore alignment and reduce pain. Because Galeazzi fractures are unstable injuries, simple casting alone is often inadequate to maintain reduction.\nTherefore, external fixation may be used in the emergency setting to stabilize the fracture temporarily, especially when there is significant soft tissue swelling or sharp fracture ends, as described in the question. External fixation helps maintain alignment while preventing further soft-tissue damage until definitive treatment is performed.\nDefinitive treatment usually involves open reduction and internal fixation (ORIF) of the radius, but in the acute emergency setting, stabilization with external fixation after reduction is appropriate.\nHence, the correct answer is (B) Check vascularity, Close Reduction, External Fixation.",
