      "explanation": "The underlying pathophysiology of pre-eclampsia is rooted in abnormal placentation during the first half of pregnancy. In a normal pregnancy, specialized placental cells called extravillous trophoblasts invade the maternal spiral arterioles in the decidua and myometrium. This invasion remodels the vessels, destroying their muscular and elastic layers and transforming them from narrow, high-resistance vessels into wide, low-resistance, high-capacitance channels. This ensures a massive increase in blood flow to the placenta to meet the demands of the growing fetus. In pre-eclampsia, this process is incomplete. There is a failure of the extravillous trophoblasts to properly invade and remodel the spiral arterioles. The vessels remain narrow and spastic, leading to placental hypoperfusion, ischemia, and hypoxia. The stressed placenta then releases anti-angiogenic factors into the maternal circulation, causing the systemic endothelial dysfunction that manifests as hypertension and proteinuria.",
