      "explanation": "The image shows a delayed intravenous urogram (IVU) demonstrating marked dilatation of the renal pelvis and calyces with narrowing at the junction where the renal pelvis meets the ureter. This appearance is typical of pelviureteric junction (PUJ) obstruction.\nPelviureteric junction obstruction occurs when there is a blockage at the junction between the renal pelvis and the proximal ureter, preventing normal drainage of urine from the kidney into the ureter. As a result, urine accumulates in the renal pelvis, leading to hydronephrosis, which appears on imaging as ballooning or dilatation of the renal pelvis and calyces with a normal or narrow ureter.\nIn the IVU image, the contrast outlines a dilated renal pelvis while the ureter distal to the obstruction is poorly visualized, which strongly suggests obstruction at the pelviureteric junction.\nOther options are less likely:\n1. Staghorn calculus would show a large branching stone occupying the renal pelvis and calyces.\n2. Putty kidney is associated with renal tuberculosis and shows a calcified, shrunken kidney.\n3. Renal cyst appears as a smooth round filling defect rather than dilated calyces.\nTherefore, the most likely diagnosis is pelviureteric junction obstruction.",
