      "correct": "B",
      "answerText": "(B) Ultrasound Doppler",
      "explanation": "A middle-aged man with acute onset pain in the lower limb raises suspicion of vascular compromise, such as arterial occlusion or deep vein thrombosis (DVT). The investigation commonly performed first in such situations is Ultrasound Doppler.\nUltrasound Doppler (Doppler ultrasonography) is a non-invasive imaging technique that uses high-frequency sound waves to evaluate blood flow in arteries and veins. It works based on the Doppler effect, where the frequency of ultrasound waves changes when reflected from moving blood cells. This allows clinicians to assess blood flow velocity, direction, and any obstruction in the vessel.\nIn cases of acute limb pain, Doppler ultrasound helps detect:\n1. Arterial blockage or reduced blood flow\n2. Deep vein thrombosis\n3. Peripheral arterial disease\nIt is usually the initial investigation of choice because it is quick, safe, cost-effective, and does not require contrast injection.\nOther options are less appropriate as first-line tests:\n4. Digital subtraction angiography is invasive and used mainly for detailed vascular imaging or intervention.\n5. Plethysmography measures volume changes in limbs but is less commonly used for acute diagnosis.\n6. MR angiography is more advanced imaging but not typically the first investigation.\nTherefore, the investigation shown is Ultrasound Doppler.",
