      "explanation": "The patient has a history of road traffic accident with inability to pass urine and blood at the urethral meatus, which are classic signs of urethral injury. In trauma cases, the most common mechanism is pelvic fracture, which is strongly associated with injury to the posterior urethra, particularly the membranous urethra.\nThe retrograde urethrogram (RGU) is the investigation of choice to identify the site of urethral injury. In the image, contrast is seen filling the anterior urethra but not passing normally into the proximal urethra, indicating a disruption at the level of the membranous urethra. This pattern is typical for posterior urethral injury associated with pelvic fractures.\nAnatomically, the membranous urethra lies between the prostatic urethra and the bulb of the penis and passes through the urogenital diaphragm. It is relatively fixed in position, making it more susceptible to shearing forces during pelvic trauma.\nOther options are less likely:\n1. Penile and spongy urethral injuries usually occur from straddle injuries.\n2. Bulbar urethral injury is also commonly due to perineal trauma rather than pelvic fractures.\nTherefore, based on the clinical features and RGU findings, the most likely site of injury is the membranous urethra.",
