      "explanation": "The image displayed is a Positron Emission Tomography-Computed Tomography (PET-CT) scan. This is a hybrid imaging modality that combines two different types of scans performed in a single session. The PET scan provides functional information by detecting the metabolic activity of tissues. The patient is injected with a radiotracer, most commonly 18F-fluorodeoxyglucose (FDG), a glucose analog. Tissues with high metabolic activity, such as cancer cells, take up more FDG and appear as hot spots (bright areas) on the PET image. The CT scan provides detailed anatomical information. The two sets of images are then fused together, allowing for the precise anatomical localization of areas of abnormal metabolic activity. This is invaluable in oncology for cancer staging, assessing treatment response, and detecting recurrence.",
