      "answerText": "(D) Consolidation with air bronchogram",
      "explanation": "The patient's symptoms are suggestive of pneumonia. The High-Resolution Computed Tomography (HRCT) of the chest shows an area of consolidation, which is the replacement of normal air-filled alveolar spaces with fluid, pus, or other material, causing the lung tissue to become opaque. The key finding within this area of consolidation is the presence of air bronchograms. This refers to the phenomenon where the air-filled bronchi remain visible as dark, branching, tubular structures against the background of the opacified lung parenchyma. This sign indicates that the pathology is within the alveoli and has not obstructed the larger airways. The combination of consolidation and air bronchograms is a classic sign of lobar pneumonia. A pleural effusion (B) would be fluid in the pleural space. A mass (A) or hernia (C) would have a different appearance.",
