      "explanation": "The patient's history is classic for sialolithiasis, which is the formation of a stone (sialolith) within a salivary gland or its duct. The symptom of swelling that worsens with eating (mealtime syndrome) is pathognomonic. When a person eats or even thinks about food, salivary flow is stimulated. If the duct is obstructed by a stone, the saliva cannot drain, causing the gland to become painfully swollen. The imaging confirms the diagnosis by showing a distinct, radio-opaque calcification located in the path of the submandibular duct (Wharton's duct), which is the most common site for salivary stones. The submandibular gland produces thick, mucinous saliva, which predisposes it to stone formation. An osteoma (C) would be part of the bone, and lymphadenitis (D) would not be related to meals in this manner.",
