      "correct": "D",
      "answerText": "(D) Chest tube placement & drainage",
      "explanation": "The patient's presentation following a high-impact motor vehicle accident—respiratory distress with absent breath sounds on one side—is a classic sign of a life-threatening chest injury, most likely a tension pneumothorax (air in the chest cavity) or a massive hemothorax (blood in the chest cavity).\nBoth conditions cause the lung on the affected (right) side to collapse. The buildup of pressure inside the chest not only prevents breathing but can also push the heart and major blood vessels to the opposite side, leading to cardiovascular collapse.\nThe immediate, life-saving intervention is to decompress the chest cavity. Chest tube placement (also known as tube thoracostomy) is the definitive procedure for this. A tube is inserted through the chest wall into the pleural space to drain the trapped air or blood. This allows the collapsed lung to re-expand and relieves the pressure on the heart, stabilizing the patient's breathing and circulation.\nWhy Other Options Are Incorrect\n(A) Immediate thoracotomy : This is a major open-chest surgery. It may be required later to repair severe internal injuries but is not the first step for stabilization.\n(B) Pleurodesis : This is a non-emergency procedure used to prevent recurrent collapsed lungs, not for treating an acute traumatic injury.\n(C) Pericardiocentesis : This procedure drains fluid from the sac around the heart and is used to treat cardiac tamponade, which presents with different clinical signs (e.g., muffled heart sounds).",
