      "explanation": "The image shows the classic radiographic signs of a small bowel obstruction (SBO) : multiple, dilated loops of small bowel (typically \\>3 cm in diameter) with prominent air-fluid levels on an erect or decubitus film. The clinical presentation of abdominal pain and vomiting is also consistent. While the patient's vitals are currently stable, a complete SBO (especially a closed-loop obstruction or one with signs of ischemia) is a surgical emergency. The initial management involves aggressive intravenous fluid resuscitation to correct dehydration and electrolyte imbalances, and nasogastric tube decompression to relieve vomiting. However, the definitive treatment for a complete or complicated SBO is an exploratory laparotomy. This allows the surgeon to identify and relieve the cause of the obstruction (e.g., adhesions, hernias, tumors) and to assess the viability of the bowel.",
