      "explanation": "A 2-year-old child with sudden-onset breathing difficulty while playing strongly suggests foreign body aspiration, which is common in young children. The chest X-ray showing unilateral hyperinflation indicates air trapping caused by a partial obstruction of a bronchus, producing a ball-valve effect where air enters during inspiration but cannot escape easily during expiration.\nClinically, children may present with sudden cough, wheezing, respiratory distress, and decreased breath sounds on the affected side. Imaging often shows unilateral hyperinflation, mediastinal shift, or localized atelectasis, depending on the degree of obstruction.\nThe definitive management is bronchoscopy to remove the foreign body. In this context, flexible bronchoscopy can be used for visualization and removal of the aspirated object, especially in diagnostic evaluation and selected therapeutic situations. It allows direct visualization of the airway and identification of the obstructing material.\nOther options are incorrect because:\n1. Acute laryngotracheitis causes stridor and barking cough.\n2. Decreased air entry is a clinical sign but not the key statement regarding management.\n3. Reactive pulmonary secretions are not the primary mechanism.\nThus, the correct statement regarding this condition is (D) Prefer way of removing is flexible bronchoscopy.",
