      "explanation": "The image shows a marbling pattern of the skin, a classic postmortem change seen during putrefaction. Marbling appears as greenish-black, branching lines along superficial veins, giving a network-like pattern on the skin.\nThis occurs due to the decomposition of blood by bacteria after death. During putrefaction, bacteria produce gases and break down hemoglobin in the blood. The hemoglobin reacts with hydrogen sulfide produced by bacteria to form sulfhemoglobin, which gives the veins a dark green or black discoloration. Because veins are distributed in a branching pattern, the discoloration produces the characteristic marbled appearance.\nMarbling usually begins around 12–24 hours after death, often first appearing over the abdomen or dependent areas, and then spreads to other parts of the body as decomposition progresses.\nOther options are incorrect:\n1. It is not due directly to sulfhaemoglobin pigment alone but bacterial decomposition.\n2. It is not non-bacterial; bacteria play a major role.\n3. It is not related to lightning injuries.\nTherefore, marbling is a postmortem change that typically develops within about 12 hours after death.",
