      "explanation": "The medication shown in the image is Mitomycin-C, a cytotoxic antineoplastic antibiotic that inhibits DNA synthesis by cross-linking DNA strands. Although it is primarily used as a chemotherapeutic drug, it also has important topical applications in surgical procedures because of its ability to inhibit fibroblast proliferation and scar formation.\nIn ENT (otolaryngology) practice, Mitomycin-C is commonly applied topically during airway surgeries to prevent excessive scar tissue formation. One of its most recognized uses is in the management of subglottic stenosis, a condition characterized by narrowing of the airway just below the vocal cords. After procedures such as endoscopic dilation or surgical correction, topical Mitomycin-C is applied to the affected area to reduce fibrosis and prevent restenosis (re-narrowing of the airway).\nThe other options are less appropriate:\n1. Inlay type-I myringoplasty involves tympanic membrane repair and does not commonly require Mitomycin-C.\n2. Post-adenoidectomy bleeding is managed with hemostatic methods.\n3. Rhino-cerebral mucormycosis is treated with antifungals like amphotericin B.\nTherefore, Mitomycin-C is most commonly used topically in subglottic stenosis management.",
