      "explanation": "The management of Primary Open-Angle Glaucoma (POAG) in a patient with bronchial asthma requires careful consideration of medication side effects. Topical beta-blockers, such as timolol, are a common first-line treatment for POAG but are relatively contraindicated in patients with asthma because they can be absorbed systemically and cause bronchoconstriction by blocking beta-2 receptors in the lungs. Therefore, an alternative first-line agent is necessary. Prostaglandin analogs, such as Latanoprost, are considered the drug of choice in this scenario. Latanoprost effectively lowers intraocular pressure by increasing the uveoscleral outflow of aqueous humor. Crucially, it has minimal systemic side effects and does not affect the respiratory system, making it a safe and highly effective option for glaucoma patients with co-existing asthma. The other options are also prostaglandins but are used for different indications (e.g., obstetrics, erectile dysfunction) and are not formulated as eye drops for glaucoma.",
