      "explanation": "The patient has developed a drug-induced acute dystonic reaction, a type of extrapyramidal symptom (EPS). This is a common side effect of dopamine D2 receptor antagonist drugs, which include many common antiemetics (like metoclopramide and prochlorperazine) and antipsychotics. These reactions are caused by a temporary imbalance between the dopaminergic (inhibitory) and cholinergic (excitatory) pathways in the basal ganglia. The blockade of dopamine receptors leads to a relative excess of cholinergic activity, resulting in involuntary muscle spasms affecting the neck (torticollis), eyes (oculogyric crisis), or trunk. The specific antidote for this condition is an anticholinergic medication, which works to restore the dopaminergic-cholinergic balance. Benzhexol (also known as trihexyphenidyl) is an anticholinergic agent used for this purpose. Other commonly used treatments include benztropine and the antihistamine diphenhydramine, which also has strong anticholinergic properties.",
