      "explanation": "The patient has recurrent chest discomfort for 72 hours relieved by glyceryl trinitrate (GTN), which strongly suggests unstable angina, a form of acute coronary syndrome (ACS). In unstable angina, troponin levels are typically normal, because there is no myocardial necrosis, but the patient still has ischemia due to partial coronary artery obstruction. The ECG may show nonspecific changes such as T-wave flattening or ST-segment changes, which further supports myocardial ischemia.\nManagement of unstable angina focuses on preventing thrombus progression and reducing the risk of myocardial infarction. In addition to antiplatelet therapy (aspirin), anticoagulation with LMWH is recommended. Low Molecular Weight Heparin (LMWH) helps prevent further clot formation by inhibiting factor Xa and thrombin, thereby reducing coronary thrombosis and improving coronary blood flow.\nThe patient is already on aspirin, statin, and beta-blocker, which are standard treatments. Therefore, the next step is adding anticoagulation with LMWH.\nOther options are less appropriate:\n1. Increasing beta-blocker does not address thrombosis.\n2. IV NTG is for persistent pain.\n3. Clopidogrel may be added but anticoagulation is essential initially.\nThus, LMWH is the next best step.",
