      "number": 116,
      "statement": "A 30-year-old man weighing 70 kg is found to have a serum sodium level of 120 mEq/L. Calculate the total sodium deficit in this patient.",
      "options": [
        "420 mEq",
        "840 mEq",
        "1400 mEq",
        "280 mEq"
      ],
      "correct": "B",
      "answerText": "(B) 840 mEq",
      "explanation": "The sodium deficit can be calculated using a standard formula that considers the total body water (TBW) and the desired change in serum sodium concentration. The formula is :\nSodium Deficit = Total Body Water (TBW) × (Desired Na⁺ - Measured Na⁺) First, we calculate the TBW, which is approximately 60% of the body weight in an adult male.\nTBW = 70 kg × 0.60 = 42 L.\nNext, we determine the desired change in sodium. The desired sodium level is typically 140 mEq/L.\nDesired change = 140 mEq/L - 120 mEq/L = 20 mEq/L.\nFinally, we calculate the deficit : Sodium Deficit = 42 L × 20 mEq/L = 840 mEq.\nThis represents the total amount of sodium required to correct the serum sodium level from 120 to 140 mEq/L. It is important to note that this deficit should be corrected slowly to avoid osmotic demyelination syndrome.",
