      "explanation": "Angiotensin-Converting Enzyme (ACE) inhibitors (and Angiotensin II Receptor Blockers, ARBs) are absolutely contraindicated during pregnancy. They are known teratogens, particularly when used in the second and third trimesters. Exposure to these drugs can cause a condition known as fetal ACE inhibitor fetopathy, which is characterized by fetal hypotension, oligohydramnios (low amniotic fluid), pulmonary hypoplasia, limb contractures, skull hypoplasia, and neonatal renal failure, which is often fatal. Therefore, any woman with hypertension who is planning to conceive must have her ACE inhibitor stopped and be switched to a pregnancy-safe alternative before attempting conception. Safe alternatives include methyldopa and labetalol, which are first-line agents for treating hypertension in pregnancy.",
