      "answerText": "(B) Hysteroscopic resection of septum",
      "explanation": "A uterine septum is a congenital Müllerian anomaly where a fibrous or muscular wall divides the uterine cavity. It is a significant cause of recurrent pregnancy loss and adverse obstetric outcomes. The modern gold standard and preferred management for a symptomatic uterine septum is hysteroscopic resection, also known as hysteroscopic metroplasty. This is a minimally invasive procedure where a hysteroscope is inserted through the cervix into the uterine cavity. Specialized instruments, such as a resectoscope or scissors, are then used under direct visualization to incise and remove the septum, thereby creating a single, unified uterine cavity. This approach has excellent success rates in improving reproductive outcomes. It has largely replaced traditional open abdominal metroplasty (D) because it avoids an abdominal incision, resulting in significantly less morbidity, faster recovery, and a lower risk of post-operative adhesions. Laparoscopy (A) may be used concurrently for surveillance to prevent uterine perforation but is not the primary resective method.",
