      "answerText": "(B) She is susceptible to chickenpox",
      "explanation": "Varicella-zoster virus (VZV) causes chickenpox (varicella) as its primary infection. After this infection, the virus becomes latent in the dorsal root ganglia. Immunity is conferred by the presence of anti-VZV antibodies. A person who is immune can later develop zoster (shingles) due to reactivation of this latent virus. This patient has a negative varicella antibody test, which means she has never had a primary infection with VZV and therefore has no immunity. As a non-immune individual, she is susceptible to contracting the primary infection, which is chickenpox, from her infected nephew. This is a high-risk situation, as primary varicella infection during pregnancy can have serious consequences for both the mother and the fetus. She is a candidate for post-exposure prophylaxis with Varicella-Zoster Immune Globulin (VZIG).",
