      "explanation": "The chest X-ray shows the classic “snowman” or “figure-of-eight” sign, which is characteristic of Total Anomalous Pulmonary Venous Connection (TAPVC), specifically the supracardiac type. In this condition, the pulmonary veins do not connect normally to the left atrium. Instead, they drain abnormally into the systemic venous circulation, usually through the superior vena cava via a vertical vein.\nOn chest X-ray, the dilated vertical vein, superior vena cava, and innominate vein form the upper portion of the snowman, while the enlarged right atrium forms the lower portion, producing the characteristic snowman or figure-8 appearance. This radiographic sign is typically seen in supracardiac TAPVC.\nClinically, infants present with cyanosis, tachypnea, and signs of heart failure because oxygenated blood from the lungs mixes with systemic venous blood before reaching the systemic circulation.\nOther options have different radiological signs:\n1. Tetralogy of Fallot → boot-shaped heart\n2. Transposition of great arteries → egg-on-side appearance\n3. Truncus arteriosus → cardiomegaly without snowman sign\nThus, the most likely diagnosis is supracardiac TAPVC (snowman heart).",
