      "correct": "C",
      "answerText": "(C) Neurons",
      "explanation": "The sensitivity of cells to ionizing radiation is described by the Law of Bergonié and Tribondeau, which states that radiosensitivity is directly proportional to the rate of cell division and inversely proportional to the degree of cell differentiation. Cells that are rapidly dividing and poorly differentiated are the most sensitive. Tissues like bone marrow, intestinal epithelium, and spermatogonia are composed of rapidly proliferating cells and are therefore highly radiosensitive. In contrast, neurons are highly specialized, fully differentiated cells that are post-mitotic (they do not divide in adulthood). This makes them one of the most radioresistant cell types in the body. While very high doses of radiation can cause neuronal damage, they are significantly less affected by conventional radiation doses compared to the rapidly renewing tissues listed in the other options.",
