      "answerText": "(D) 22%",
      "explanation": "This calculation is based on Chargaff's rules of base pairing in double-stranded DNA. The rules state that the amount of adenine (A) is always equal to the amount of thymine (T), and the amount of guanine (G) is always equal to the amount of cytosine (C). The total percentage of all four bases must equal 100%.\n1. Given that Adenine (A) = 28%.\n2. According to the rules, Thymine (T) must also be 28%.\n3. The total percentage of A and T is A + T = 28% + 28% = 56%.\n4. The remaining percentage must be the sum of Guanine (G) and Cytosine (C). So, G + C = 100% - 56% = 44%.\n5. Since the amount of G is equal to the amount of C, we can divide the remaining percentage by 2 to find the amount of Cytosine.\n6. Cytosine (C) = 44% / 2 = 22%.\nTherefore, the amount of cytosine present in the DNA sample will be 22%.",
