      "explanation": "Food poisoning with a very short incubation period (less than 6 hours) and vomiting as the predominant symptom is characteristic of intoxication by a pre-formed bacterial toxin. Staphylococcus aureus is a classic cause of this type of food poisoning. The bacterium contaminates food (often protein-rich foods like meats, salads, and cream-filled pastries) and, if left at room temperature, multiplies and produces a heat-stable enterotoxin. Ingestion of the food containing this pre-formed toxin leads to a rapid onset of symptoms, typically within 1 to 6 hours, with nausea, profuse vomiting, and abdominal cramps being the main features. Diarrhea may also occur but is less prominent. In contrast, infections with Salmonella typically have a longer incubation period (12-72 hours) and cause a diarrheal illness. C. perfringens causes diarrhea with an 8-16 hour incubation.",
