      "correct": "A",
      "answerText": "(A) Immuno chromatography assays",
      "explanation": "The detection of Hepatitis B surface antigen (HBsAg) is the primary method for screening for and diagnosing Hepatitis B virus (HBV) infection. Modern rapid diagnostic tests for HBsAg are most commonly based on the principle of lateral flow immuno chromatography. These assays are qualitative, single-use devices (often in a cassette or strip format) that provide a result in minutes. The test strip contains a membrane impregnated with anti-HBsAg antibodies. When the patient's sample (serum, plasma, or whole blood) is applied, if HBsAg is present, it will bind to these antibodies as it migrates along the strip. This antigen-antibody complex is then captured at a test line that contains another set of immobilized anti-HBsAg antibodies, forming a visible colored line. While laboratory-based tests like ELISA and chemiluminescence are also used for quantitative detection, the rapid tests are based on immuno chromatography.",
