      "correct": "B",
      "answerText": "(B) Pili (fimbriae)",
      "explanation": "A critical first step in the pathogenesis of infection by Neisseria gonorrhoeae is its attachment to the mucosal epithelial cells of the urogenital tract, pharynx, or rectum. This initial adherence is primarily mediated by hair-like appendages on the bacterial surface called pili (also known as fimbriae). The protein subunit of the pilus, called pilin, binds to specific receptors (such as CD46) on the host cell surface. This attachment allows the gonococci to resist being washed away by urine or mucosal secretions and establishes a foothold for subsequent invasion and infection. Pili are also crucial for twitching motility and genetic exchange. The ability of N. gonorrhoeae to undergo antigenic variation of its pilin protein helps it evade the host immune response. Other virulence factors like Opa proteins mediate tighter binding after the initial attachment by pili.",
