      "explanation": "The management algorithm for supraventricular tachycardia (SVT) is dictated by the patient's hemodynamic stability. A patient is considered unstable if they exhibit signs of poor perfusion, such as hypotension, altered mental status, signs of shock, or acute heart failure. In any tachyrhythmia, the presence of hemodynamic instability is a medical emergency that requires immediate restoration of a normal sinus rhythm. The treatment of choice in this situation is synchronized electrical cardioversion. This procedure involves delivering a timed, low-energy electrical shock to the heart, which is synchronized with the R-wave of the ECG. This depolarizes the entire myocardium, interrupts the re-entrant circuit of the SVT, and allows the heart's natural pacemaker (the sinus node) to regain control. Pharmacological treatments like IV diltiazem (B) or beta-blockers (D) are reserved for hemodynamically stable patients.",
