      "explanation": "Esophageal manometry is the gold standard test used to evaluate esophageal motility disorders. According to the Chicago Classification, achalasia is divided into three types based on manometric findings.\nType 3 achalasia (spastic achalasia) is characterized by premature or spastic contractions in the distal esophagus along with impaired relaxation of the lower esophageal sphincter (LES). On manometry, it shows very high distal contractile integral (DCI) values, often greater than 450 mmHg·s·cm, indicating strong spastic contractions.\nAlthough panesophageal pressurization can be seen, the distinguishing feature of Type 3 achalasia is the presence of abnormal spastic contractions with preserved or excessive contractile activity in the esophageal body.\nOther options are less consistent with the findings:\n1. Type 1 achalasia : Minimal contractility and no significant pressurization (classic achalasia).\n2. Type 2 achalasia : Panesophageal pressurization but without strong distal contractions.\n3. Jackhammer esophagus: Shows extremely strong contractions but normal LES relaxation, unlike achalasia.\nTherefore, the findings of high contractile integrity with panesophageal pressurization are most consistent with Type 3 achalasia (spastic achalasia).",
