      "explanation": "The APGAR score is a rapid assessment tool used to evaluate the clinical status of a newborn infant at 1 and 5 minutes after birth. It scores five parameters (Appearance, Pulse, Grimace, Activity, Respiration) on a scale of 0 to 2 each, for a maximum total score of 10. The score is used to guide the need for resuscitation. A score is interpreted as follows :\n1. 7-10 : Normal / Good condition. The infant is adapting well.\n2. 4-6 : Mildly to moderately depressed. The infant may require some assistance, such as stimulation and supplemental oxygen.\n3. 0-3 : Severely depressed. The infant is in critical condition and requires immediate and intensive resuscitation, including positive pressure ventilation and possibly chest compressions and medications.\nAn APGAR score of 3 at 1 minute clearly indicates that the neonate is severely depressed and in urgent need of life-saving resuscitation measures to support their breathing and circulation.",
