      "correct": "A",
      "answerText": "(A) MCA Doppler peak systolic volume",
      "explanation": "In an Rh-sensitized pregnancy, maternal anti-D antibodies cross the placenta and destroy fetal red blood cells, leading to fetal anemia. The severity of this anemia must be monitored to guide interventions like intrauterine transfusions. In modern obstetrics, the best and standard method for non-invasively assessing the degree of fetal anemia is by measuring the Peak Systolic Velocity (PSV) of the fetal Middle Cerebral Artery (MCA) using Doppler ultrasound. In an anemic fetus, the blood is thinner (less viscous), and the heart compensates by increasing its output. This results in a higher velocity of blood flow in the cerebral arteries. A high MCA-PSV (above 1.5 multiples of the median for gestational age) is a highly sensitive and specific indicator of moderate to severe fetal anemia. This non-invasive technique has largely replaced older, invasive methods like amniocentesis (to measure bilirubin levels) and fetal blood sampling (cordocentesis).",
