      "explanation": "A septate uterus is a congenital Müllerian anomaly that is a common cause of recurrent pregnancy loss. The gold standard and best treatment option for a symptomatic uterine septum is transcervical hysteroscopic resection of the septum (also known as hysteroscopic metroplasty). This is a minimally invasive surgical procedure. A hysteroscope is passed through the cervix into the uterine cavity, and the septum is incised or resected under direct visualization using instruments like hysteroscopic scissors, a resectoscope, or a laser. This procedure effectively unifies the uterine cavity, significantly improving subsequent pregnancy outcomes. It has replaced older, more invasive procedures like the Jones and Tompkins metroplasties, which were open abdominal surgeries that required a uterine incision (hysterotomy). The hysteroscopic approach has much lower morbidity, a faster recovery, and avoids the risk of uterine scarring and subsequent rupture in pregnancy.",
