      "number": 288,
      "statement": "What percentage of oxygen is provided during mouth-to-mouth respiration?",
      "options": [
        "10%",
        "16%",
        "21%",
        "100%"
      ],
      "correct": "B",
      "answerText": "(B) 16%",
      "explanation": "During resuscitation, mouth-to-mouth breathing is a method of providing rescue breaths. The air we breathe in (inspired air) contains approximately 21% oxygen. When we exhale, our bodies have used some of this oxygen for metabolism, and we release carbon dioxide as a waste product. However, not all the oxygen is extracted. The air that a rescuer exhales contains a lower concentration of oxygen and a higher concentration of carbon dioxide compared to ambient air. Exhaled air typically contains about 16% to 17% oxygen. While this is less than the 21% in room air, it is still a sufficient concentration of oxygen to sustain life and provide oxygenation to a non-breathing victim until more advanced airway management and higher concentrations of oxygen can be provided. Delivering 100% oxygen would require a specialized breathing circuit and a pure oxygen source.",
      "pages": [
        488,
        557
      ],
