      "statement": "Bainbridge reflex causes which of the following?",
      "options": [
        "Bradycardia",
        "Increased cardiac output",
        "Decreased venous return",
        "Increased heart rate"
      ],
      "correct": "D",
      "answerText": "(D) Increased heart rate",
      "explanation": "The Bainbridge reflex, also known as the atrial reflex, is a physiological mechanism that results in an increase in heart rate in response to an increase in venous return and right atrial pressure. When venous return to the heart increases, the walls of the right atrium are stretched. This stretching stimulates low-pressure baroreceptors (stretch receptors) located in the atrial walls. The afferent signals from these receptors travel via the vagus nerve to the cardiovascular center in the medulla oblongata. The medullary centers then respond by decreasing parasympathetic (vagal) tone and increasing sympathetic tone to the sinoatrial (SA) node. This results in a reflexive tachycardia, or an increased heart rate. This reflex helps to prevent the pooling of blood in the venous system by accelerating the heart's pumping action to match the increased venous return.",
