      "explanation": "The peripheral chemoreceptors, located in the carotid and aortic bodies, are responsible for sensing changes in the partial pressures of oxygen (PO₂) and carbon dioxide (PCO₂), and the pH of the arterial blood. The glomus (type I) cells within these bodies are the primary sensory elements. The cellular mechanism for sensing hypoxia (low PO₂) involves a specific type of oxygen-sensitive potassium (K⁺) channel in the glomus cell membrane. When the arterial PO₂ falls, these K⁺ channels close. The closure of these channels reduces the outward flow of potassium ions, which leads to depolarization of the cell membrane. This depolarization then opens voltage-gated calcium channels (A), causing an influx of calcium. The rise in intracellular calcium triggers the release of neurotransmitters (like dopamine), which then stimulate the afferent nerve fibers that project to the respiratory centers in the brainstem, leading to an increase in ventilation.",
