      "correct": "A",
      "answerText": "(A) GFR (Glomerular Filtration Rate)",
      "explanation": "Tubuloglomerular feedback (TGF) is a crucial intrarenal mechanism that contributes to the autoregulation of renal blood flow and glomerular filtration rate (GFR). This feedback loop links the GFR to the composition of the tubular fluid at the end of the loop of Henle. The specialized cells of the macula densa, located in the distal tubule, sense the flow rate and the concentration of sodium chloride in the tubular fluid. If the GFR increases, more fluid and salt are delivered to the macula densa. In response, the macula densa cells release signaling molecules (like ATP and adenosine) that cause vasoconstriction of the afferent arteriole. This afferent constriction reduces glomerular capillary pressure and brings the GFR back down towards its normal set point. This negative feedback system ensures that the GFR remains relatively constant despite fluctuations in systemic blood pressure.",
