      "explanation": "Decorticate rigidity (or posturing) is a stereotypical posture that occurs with specific types of severe brain injury. It is caused by a lesion in the cerebral hemispheres that is superior to the red nucleus in the midbrain. This lesion disconnects the cerebral cortex from the brainstem. The posture is characterized by flexion of the upper limbs (arms flexed and adducted, wrists and fingers flexed) and extension of the lower limbs. This occurs because the rubrospinal tract, which originates from the red nucleus and facilitates upper limb flexion, is left intact and unopposed by cortical input. The vestibulospinal tract causes extension in all limbs, but its effect on the upper limbs is overridden by the rubrospinal tract. In decerebrate rigidity, the lesion is below the red nucleus, so both upper and lower limbs are extended. Decerebrate rigidity is generally considered a more severe injury, and the associated extensor rigidity is more pronounced than in decorticate posturing.",
