      "explanation": "Oseltamivir (Tamiflu) is an antiviral drug that is active against both influenza A and influenza B viruses. Its mechanism of action is the selective inhibition of the viral enzyme neuraminidase. Neuraminidase is a glycoprotein located on the surface of the influenza virus. This enzyme plays a critical role in the final stage of the viral replication cycle. It cleaves the sialic acid residues that bind the newly formed viral particles to the surface of the infected host cell. This cleavage is necessary for the release of the progeny virions, allowing them to spread and infect other respiratory epithelial cells. By inhibiting neuraminidase, oseltamivir prevents the release of new viruses from the infected cell, thus halting the spread of the infection within the respiratory tract. To be effective, the drug must be started early in the course of the illness, typically within 48 hours of symptom onset.",
