      "explanation": "Colchicine is an anti-inflammatory drug mainly used in gout and certain autoinflammatory conditions. It works by binding to tubulin and inhibiting microtubule polymerization.\nColchicine inhibits neutrophil recruitment by disrupting microtubule-dependent processes such as chemotaxis, adhesion, and degranulation. Since neutrophils play a central role in the inflammatory response to monosodium urate crystals, this mechanism reduces inflammation during acute gout attacks.\nIt also causes metaphase arrest. Microtubules are essential for mitotic spindle formation during cell division. By preventing spindle assembly, colchicine arrests cells in metaphase. This property has been used in cytogenetic studies to visualize chromosomes.\nAlthough colchicine does not directly lower serum uric acid levels, it is useful in urate-lowering therapy. When drugs like allopurinol are started, rapid shifts in uric acid levels can trigger acute gout flares. Colchicine is given prophylactically to prevent these attacks.\nTherefore, all statements are correct, making (D) All of the above the best answer.",
