      "explanation": "Isolation precautions are used in hospitals to prevent the transmission of infectious agents. Contact isolation is used for pathogens that are spread by direct or indirect contact with the patient or their environment. This includes multidrug-resistant organisms like Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus (MRSA) and vancomycin-resistant enterococci (VRE), as well as C. difficile and scabies. Diphtheria (cutaneous form) also requires contact precautions. In contrast, Mumps is a viral illness that is transmitted via large respiratory droplets. Therefore, patients with mumps require droplet isolation precautions, not contact isolation. Droplet precautions involve wearing a surgical mask when working within close proximity to the patient, in addition to standard precautions. This is different from contact precautions, which emphasize the use of gowns and gloves to prevent transmission via contact.",
