      "explanation": "This clinical scenario provides several key clues pointing to a specific parasitic infection. The patient presents with pulmonary symptoms, including a chronic cough and rusty or brownish sputum, which suggests the presence of blood and eggs. The history of travel to an endemic area (China) and the consumption of raw or undercooked freshwater crabs is the definitive epidemiological link. This is the classic mode of transmission for the lung fluke, Paragonimus westermani. Humans become infected by eating crabs or crayfish containing the metacercariae. The larvae excyst, penetrate the intestine, and migrate to the lungs, where they mature into adult flukes and form fibrous capsules. The eggs laid by the adult worms erode into the bronchioles and are coughed up in the sputum, causing the characteristic rusty color.",
