      "explanation": "The gold standard serological test for the diagnosis of leptospirosis is the Microscopic Agglutination Test (MAT). This is a complex and reference laboratory test that is considered the most specific method for diagnosing the infection. The MAT involves testing the patient's serum against a panel of live Leptospira serovars that are representative of the local epidemiology. The serum is serially diluted and mixed with the live organisms. If specific antibodies are present in the patient's serum, they will bind to the spirochetes, causing them to agglutinate (clump together) and lose their motility. This agglutination is then observed under a dark-field microscope. A four-fold or greater rise in the antibody titer between acute and convalescent phase serum samples is considered diagnostic of a recent infection.",
