      "explanation": "A confounding factor (or confounder) is a third variable in an epidemiological study that can distort the true relationship between an exposure and an outcome. For a variable to be a confounder, it must meet three specific criteria :\n1. It must be a risk factor for the disease (outcome) among the unexposed.\n2. It must be associated with the exposure being studied in the source population.\n3. It must not be an intermediate step in the causal pathway between the exposure and the disease.\nWhen a study is conducted, if this confounding factor is distributed unequally between the exposed (study) and unexposed (control) groups, it can create a spurious association or mask a true one. For example, in a study of alcohol and lung cancer, smoking is a confounder because it is associated with alcohol use, is an independent risk factor for lung cancer, and its prevalence differs between drinkers and non-drinkers.",
