      "explanation": "Stridor is a high-pitched, harsh sound produced by turbulent airflow through a partially obstructed airway. The character of the stridor can help to localize the level of the obstruction. Inspiratory stridor is characteristic of an extrathoracic airway obstruction, specifically at or above the level of the vocal cords (the glottis). Lesions in the supraglottic region, such as epiglottitis or laryngomalacia, are classic causes. During inspiration, the negative pressure generated in the airway tends to collapse the floppy supraglottic tissues inward, causing the obstruction and the characteristic inspiratory sound. In contrast, an intrathoracic obstruction (e.g., in the trachea or bronchus) typically causes expiratory stridor or wheezing, as the airway narrows during the positive pressure of expiration. A fixed obstruction, such as subglottic stenosis, often causes biphasic stridor (heard during both inspiration and expiration).",
