      "answerText": "(D) Frontalis suspension surgery",
      "explanation": "The choice of surgical procedure to correct congenital ptosis (drooping of the upper eyelid) depends primarily on the function of the levator palpebrae superioris muscle, which is the main muscle responsible for elevating the eyelid. In cases where there is poor levator function (typically defined as less than 4-5 mm of eyelid excursion from downgaze to upgaze), procedures that rely on strengthening the levator muscle itself, such as a levator muscle resection (A), are unlikely to be successful. In these situations, the preferred surgical approach is a frontalis suspension surgery, also known as a frontalis sling. This procedure bypasses the weak levator muscle and couples the movement of the eyelid to the frontalis muscle (the muscle that raises the eyebrows). A sling material (either autologous fascia lata from the thigh or a synthetic material like silicone) is passed from the tarsal plate of the eyelid up to the frontalis muscle. This allows the child to lift their eyelid by raising their eyebrow.",
