      "explanation": "Acute angle-closure glaucoma (acute congestive glaucoma) is an ophthalmologic emergency characterized by a sudden, severe rise in intraocular pressure (IOP). The immediate medical management aims to rapidly lower the IOP using a combination of medications. Key agents include :\n1. Acetazolamide : A systemic carbonic anhydrase inhibitor that rapidly and powerfully suppresses the production of aqueous humor by the ciliary body, thereby reducing the inflow of fluid into the eye.\n2. Pilocarpine : A topical miotic (cholinergic agonist) that constricts the pupil. This pupillary constriction pulls the peripheral iris away from the trabecular meshwork, physically opening the blocked drainage angle and increasing aqueous outflow.\nThis combination of decreasing aqueous production (acetazolamide) and increasing aqueous outflow (pilocarpine) is a cornerstone of initial therapy. Topical beta-blockers and alpha-agonists are also used, along with systemic hyperosmotic agents like mannitol if the IOP is very high. Atropine (A), a mydriatic, is absolutely contraindicated as it would worsen the angle closure.",
