      "explanation": "Brown-skinned syndrome results from the hemisection (damage to one half) of the spinal cord. This injury leads to a distinct pattern of neurological deficits below the level of the lesion, based on the specific tracts that are damaged.\n1. Ipsilateral (same side) deficits : The injury damages the dorsal columns and the corticospinal tract. The dorsal columns (fasciculus gracilis and cuneatus) carry sensory information for fine touch, vibration, and proprioception (joint position sense) without crossing in the spinal cord. Therefore, there is a loss of these sensations on the same side as the lesion. Damage to the corticospinal tract, which has already crossed in the medulla, causes ipsilateral spastic paralysis.\n2. Contralateral (opposite side) deficits : The injury damages the spinothalamic tract. Since the fibers of this tract (carrying pain and temperature sensation) cross to the opposite side shortly after entering the cord, there is a loss of pain and temperature sensation on the side opposite the lesion.",
