      "correct": "C",
      "answerText": "(C) Carbon dioxide",
      "explanation": "The primary and most powerful stimulus for regulating moment-to-moment breathing is the arterial partial pressure of carbon dioxide (PaCO₂). This regulation is mediated by the central chemoreceptors, which are located on the ventral surface of the medulla in the brainstem. These chemoreceptors are not directly sensitive to CO₂ itself, but rather to the concentration of hydrogen ions (H⁺) in the surrounding cerebrospinal fluid (CSF). Carbon dioxide from the blood readily diffuses across the blood-brain barrier into the CSF. There, it is converted by carbonic anhydrase into carbonic acid, which then dissociates into H⁺ and bicarbonate. The resulting increase in H⁺ concentration stimulates the central chemoreceptors, which in turn send powerful excitatory signals to the respiratory centers (DRG and VRG), leading to an increase in the rate and depth of breathing to blow off the excess CO₂. This negative feedback loop maintains arterial PCO₂ within a very narrow range.",
