      "explanation": "The influenza vaccine is unique among routine vaccines in that it is recommended to be administered every year. There are two main reasons for this. First, the immunity conferred by the influenza vaccine wanes over time, and annual revaccination is needed to maintain protection. Second, and more importantly, the influenza virus is constantly evolving through processes called antigenic drift and antigenic shift. Antigenic drift involves small, gradual changes in the virus's surface antigens (hemagglutinin and neuraminidase). These changes result in new viral strains emerging each year that the immune system may not recognize from previous infections or vaccinations. Therefore, the composition of the influenza vaccine must be updated annually to match the strains that are predicted to be circulating in the upcoming flu season. This requirement for an annual update is specific to the influenza vaccine.",
