      "explanation": "In a newborn, screening for critical congenital heart disease is often done by comparing pre-ductal and post-ductal oxygen saturation. This is because in certain heart defects, a right-to-left shunt can occur through a patent ductus arteriosus (PDA), delivering deoxygenated blood to the descending aorta. The pre-ductal saturation represents the oxygen level of the blood that is delivered to the brain and the arms before the point where the ductus arteriosus joins the aorta. The arteries that supply the right arm (the brachiocephalic and right subclavian arteries) always arise from the aorta proximal to the ductus arteriosus. Therefore, placing a pulse oximeter on the right hand or wrist provides a reliable measurement of pre-ductal saturation. In contrast, the left arm and both lower limbs are supplied by the aorta distal to the ductus arteriosus, so a probe placed on these sites would measure post-ductal saturation.",
