      "answerText": "(C) Proximal 1/3rd",
      "explanation": "The scaphoid bone of the wrist has a tenuous and unique blood supply. The majority of its blood supply (70-80%) enters through small foramina in the dorsal ridge, located in its distal half, and then flows in a retrograde fashion to supply the proximal part of the bone. The proximal pole (proximal 1/3rd) has a very limited or non-existent direct blood supply. When the scaphoid is fractured, particularly through its waist (the middle 1/3rd), this retrograde blood supply to the proximal fragment can be completely disrupted. This interruption of blood flow makes the proximal pole highly susceptible to developing avascular necrosis (AVN), a condition where the bone tissue dies due to a lack of blood supply. The risk of AVN increases significantly with more proximal fractures. This complication can lead to non-union of the fracture, collapse of the bone, and subsequent degenerative arthritis of the wrist.",
