      "answerText": "(A) BIS (Bispectral Index)",
      "explanation": "While clinical signs (heart rate, blood pressure, movement) are traditionally used to assess the depth of anesthesia, they can be unreliable. Processed electroencephalogram (EEG) monitors have been developed to provide a more objective measure of the hypnotic component of anesthesia. The most common and widely used of these is the Bispectral Index (BIS) monitor. The BIS monitor uses a sensor placed on the patient's forehead to record EEG data. It then applies a proprietary algorithm to process this complex EEG information and convert it into a single, dimensionless number ranging from 0 (complete cortical suppression) to 100 (fully awake). A BIS value between 40 and 60 is generally considered indicative of an adequate depth of general anesthesia for surgery, with a reduced risk of intraoperative awareness. Capnography monitors ventilation, not anesthetic depth.",
