      "explanation": "Dubin-Johnson syndrome is a rare, benign, autosomal recessive disorder characterized by a chronic, predominantly conjugated hyperbilirubinemia. The underlying genetic defect is a mutation in the gene that codes for the Multidrug Resistance-Associated Protein 2 (MRP2), also known as the canalicular multispecific organic anion transporter (cMOAT). This protein is an ATP-dependent efflux pump located on the apical (canalicular) membrane of hepatocytes. Its normal function is to actively transport conjugated bilirubin and a variety of other organic anions from the hepatocyte into the bile canaliculus for excretion. In Dubin-Johnson syndrome, the absence of a functional MRP2 protein leads to impaired biliary excretion of conjugated bilirubin. This causes the conjugated bilirubin to reflux back into the bloodstream, resulting in jaundice. A pathognomonic feature is the deposition of a dark, melanin-like pigment in the liver, giving it a black appearance.",
