      "correct": "A",
      "answerText": "(A) Barium",
      "explanation": "Radiological contrast agents are used to enhance the visibility of internal structures. They are broadly classified based on their properties. Iodinated contrast agents, such as iohexol and iopamidol (which are non-ionic, low-osmolar agents), and Gastrografin (which is a high-osmolar, ionic agent containing diatrizoate meglumine and diatrizoate sodium), are all water-soluble compounds. They are used for a wide range of applications, including intravenous urography, angiography, CT scans, and gastrointestinal studies. In contrast, barium sulfate is an inert, metallic salt that is completely insoluble in water. It is used exclusively for imaging the gastrointestinal tract. It is administered orally or rectally as a suspension. Because it is not absorbed, it provides excellent opacification of the GI lumen. Its insolubility means it is contraindicated if a GI perforation is suspected, as leakage into the peritoneum can cause severe peritonitis.",
