      "explanation": "Coumadin is the brand name of Warfarin, an oral anticoagulant that requires regular monitoring of prothrombin time (PT), expressed as the international normalized ratio (INR). Warfarin works by inhibiting vitamin K–dependent synthesis of clotting factors II, VII, IX, and X in the liver. Because its anticoagulant effect can vary widely due to diet, drug interactions, liver function, and genetics, careful monitoring is essential to maintain therapeutic efficacy while preventing bleeding complications.\nIf the INR is too low, there is a risk of thrombosis; if too high, there is a risk of serious bleeding. Therefore, periodic PT/INR testing guides dose adjustments.\nAspirin (A) affects platelet function and does not require PT/INR monitoring. Lepirudin (B), a direct thrombin inhibitor, is monitored using activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT), not PT/INR. Digoxin (C) is a cardiac glycoside used in heart failure and arrhythmias and requires serum drug level monitoring, not coagulation tests.\nThus, Coumadin (warfarin) is the drug that necessitates regular PT/INR monitoring.",
