      "explanation": "Dr. Edmond Locard was a French criminologist and a pioneer in the field of forensic science. He is most famous for formulating the basic principle of forensic science known as Locard's Exchange Principle. This principle states that every contact leaves a trace. It posits that whenever two objects come into contact, there is a mutual exchange of matter between them.  This means a perpetrator of a crime will bring something into the crime scene and leave with something from it, and both can be used as forensic evidence. For example, fibers from a suspect's clothing might be found on a victim, and hairs from the victim might be found on the suspect. This theory of exchange is the fundamental concept behind the collection and analysis of trace evidence in criminal investigations. Fingerprint study was advanced by Sir Francis Galton , stature estimation by Karl Pearson , and identification by body measurements (anthropometry) by Alphonse Bertillon.",
