      "correct": "C",
      "answerText": "(C) In minutes",
      "explanation": "Hyperacute rejection is the most rapid and devastating form of organ transplant rejection. It occurs almost immediately, typically within minutes to hours after the transplantation procedure, as soon as the blood supply to the transplanted organ is restored.  This type of rejection is a Type II hypersensitivity reaction. It is mediated by pre-existing recipient antibodies that are specific for antigens on the donor organ's endothelium. These are typically ABO blood group antibodies or anti-HLA antibodies that the recipient has developed from previous blood transfusions, pregnancies, or prior transplants. The binding of these antibodies to the donor endothelium activates the complement and coagulation cascades, leading to widespread thrombosis, ischemia, and rapid, irreversible necrosis of the graft. Due to pre-transplant cross-matching, hyperacute rejection is now very rare. Acute rejection occurs within days to weeks , while chronic rejection occurs over months to years.",
