      "explanation": "Cutaneous larva migrans, also known as creeping eruption, is a parasitic skin infection characterized by an intensely pruritic, erythematous, and serpiginous (snake-like) track that moves over time. The condition is caused by the accidental penetration of human skin by the filariform larvae of animal hookworms. The most common causative agent is Ancylostoma braziliense, the hookworm of dogs and cats. Humans become infected when they walk barefoot or lie on soil or sand contaminated with the feces of infected animals. The larvae penetrate the skin but are unable to complete their life cycle in humans because they lack the necessary collagenase enzymes to break through the basement membrane of the dermis. They are therefore trapped in the epidermis and wander, creating the characteristic migratory tracks. Necator americanus is a human hookworm that can penetrate the skin but then migrates to the lungs and intestines. Toxocara canis causes visceral or ocular larva migrans. Strongyloides can cause a different condition called larva currens.",
