      "correct": "A",
      "answerText": "(A) Cytomegalovirus (CMV)",
      "explanation": "Patients who undergo solid organ transplantation, such as a kidney transplant, require long-term immunosuppressive therapy to prevent graft rejection. This chronic state of immunosuppression makes them highly susceptible to a wide range of opportunistic infections. Among viral pathogens, Cytomegalovirus (CMV) is one of the most significant and common causes of morbidity and mortality in these patients. Infection can occur as a primary infection (if a CMV-negative recipient receives an organ from a CMV-positive donor), a reactivation of the recipient's latent CMV, or a superinfection with a new strain. CMV disease can manifest as a systemic febrile illness, or it can directly affect the transplanted organ (CMV nephritis) or other organ systems, causing pneumonitis, colitis, or retinitis. Because it is so common and can have severe consequences, routine monitoring for CMV viremia and prophylactic or pre-emptive antiviral therapy (e.g., with valganciclovir) is a standard part of post-transplant care. While bacterial infections also occur, CMV is the most characteristic viral pathogen in this setting.",
