      "explanation": "Cytotoxic drugs (antineoplastic or chemotherapy drugs) and expired pharmaceutical products are classified as hazardous biomedical waste. These substances are chemically reactive and potentially genotoxic, carcinogenic, or teratogenic, posing a significant risk to public health and the environment if not disposed of properly. The recommended and most effective method for the final disposal of this type of waste is high-temperature incineration. Incineration at temperatures typically above 850°C ensures the complete thermal destruction and decomposition of the active chemical compounds, rendering them inert and non-toxic. The resulting ash can then be safely disposed of in a secured landfill. Autoclaving is a sterilization method using steam, which is ineffective for chemical waste. Simply dumping or disposing in a standard landfill is prohibited as it would lead to the contamination of soil and groundwater. Therefore, secure incineration is the mandated method for destroying cytotoxic and expired drugs to prevent environmental contamination and public exposure.",
