      "explanation": "In epidemiology, different study designs use different units of observation and analysis. In most study designs, such as case-control studies or cohort studies, the unit of analysis is the individual (e.g., a patient or a case). However, an ecological study (also known as a correlational study) is unique in that the unit of analysis is not the individual but an entire population or a group of people. These studies examine the relationship between an exposure and an outcome by using aggregate data for a population, rather than data on individual members. For example, an ecological study might correlate the average per capita cigarette sales in different countries with the age-adjusted mortality rates from lung cancer in those same countries. The key characteristic is that data is analyzed at the group level, making population the correct study unit. A major limitation of this design is the ecological fallacy—an association observed at the group level may not hold true for individuals.",
