      "explanation": "Sensitivity is a measure of a screening test's ability to correctly identify individuals who truly have the disease. It is calculated as the proportion of true positives among all individuals who actually have the disease. The formula for sensitivity is :\nSensitivity = (True Positives) / (True Positives + False Negatives)\nIn this problem :\nThe total number of people who truly have the disease (as determined by the gold standard test) is 100. This is the denominator (True Positives + False Negatives).\nThe number of people who tested positive with the screening test is 90. This represents the True Positives.\nWe can infer that 10 people who had the disease were missed by the screening test (100 total diseased - 90 who tested positive = 10 False Negatives).\nTherefore, the sensitivity of the screening test is calculated as :\nSensitivity = 90 / (90 + 10) = 90 / 100.\nThis means the test correctly identifies 90% of all people who actually have diabetes mellitus.",
