      "explanation": "For the diagnosis and staging of pancreatic cancer, particularly tumors in the head of the pancreas, Endoscopic Ultrasound (EUS) is considered the most sensitive and accurate imaging modality. EUS involves passing an endoscope with a high-frequency ultrasound transducer at its tip into the stomach and duodenum. This allows for high-resolution imaging of the pancreas from a very close proximity, without interference from bowel gas. EUS is superior to CT and MRI for detecting small tumors (<2 cm), assessing the depth of tumor invasion, and evaluating local lymph node involvement and vascular invasion (T and N staging). A crucial advantage of EUS is that it also allows for EUS-guided fine-needle aspiration (FNA), which can be performed during the same procedure to obtain a tissue diagnosis. This combination of superior imaging and the ability to obtain a cytological sample makes EUS the best single investigation for both diagnosing and staging pancreatic head adenocarcinoma. ERCP is more therapeutic for relieving biliary obstruction.",
