      "answerText": "(B) Anterior (frontal)",
      "explanation": "Fontanelles are the soft, membranous gaps between the cranial bones in an infant's skull, which allow for the growth of the brain and the molding of the head during birth. There are six fontanelles at birth. The posterior fontanelle is triangular and typically closes early, by about 2 to 3 months of age. The anterolateral (sphenoidal) fontanelles on the sides of the skull usually close by about 6 months of age. The posterolateral (mastoid) fontanelles close later, between 6 and 18 months. The anterior fontanelle is the largest, diamond-shaped fontanelle, commonly known as the soft spot. It is the last one to close, with the process of ossification typically being completed between 18 and 24 months of age. Its persistence beyond this age may indicate an underlying medical condition. Because it remains open for the longest period, the anterior fontanelle is a crucial landmark for clinical assessment in infants.",
