      "explanation": "Dilatation and curettage (D&C) is a gynecological procedure in which the cervix is dilated and the endometrial lining of the uterus is scraped or suctioned. It is commonly performed for diagnostic purposes, such as evaluating abnormal uterine bleeding, or for therapeutic reasons, including incomplete abortion.\nHowever, D&C is contraindicated in patients with active pelvic inflammatory disease (PID). PID is an infection of the upper genital tract, including the uterus, fallopian tubes, and ovaries. Performing D&C in the presence of active infection may worsen the condition by spreading the infection further into the pelvic cavity or bloodstream, potentially leading to serious complications such as sepsis or tubo-ovarian abscess.\nEndometriosis is not an absolute contraindication to D&C, and ectopic pregnancy requires different management but is not itself a contraindication in the same way as active infection.\nTherefore, D&C should be avoided in patients with active pelvic inflammatory disease.\nWhich chromosomal pattern is most commonly associated with true hermaphroditism?",
