      "answerText": "(A) Cold temperature",
      "explanation": "Cutis marmorata is a common, benign skin condition characterized by a mottled, reticulated, or lace-like pattern of bluish-red discoloration on the skin. It is a physiological vasomotor response that occurs upon exposure to cold temperatures. The pattern is caused by a differential response of the cutaneous microvasculature to the cold. The small arterioles constrict, leading to sluggish blood flow and deoxygenation in the superficial venous plexuses (the blue/purple lines), while the capillary beds in the center of the loops may remain dilated or be better perfused (the pale areas). This creates the characteristic net-like appearance. It is most often seen in infants and young children due to their immature autonomic nervous system, but it can also occur in adults. The condition is transient and completely disappears upon rewarming the skin. It is not a rash and does not involve inflammation.",
