      "explanation": "The Malpighian layer, also known as the germinative layer of the epidermis, refers to the actively dividing portion of the epidermis responsible for regeneration of the skin. It is composed of two layers: the stratum basale (basal layer) and the stratum spinosum (prickle cell layer).\nThe stratum basale is the deepest layer of the epidermis. It consists of a single row of cuboidal or columnar cells that continuously undergo mitosis to produce new keratinocytes. These newly formed cells gradually move upward into the stratum spinosum.\nThe stratum spinosum lies just above the basal layer and contains several layers of polygonal cells connected by desmosomes, giving them a spiny appearance under the microscope. These cells provide strength and flexibility to the skin.\nThe stratum granulosum and stratum lucidum are more superficial layers and are not part of the Malpighian layer. Thus, the Malpighian layer consists of the stratum basale and stratum spinosum.",
