      "explanation": "The Modified Mallampati classification is a simple, non-invasive bedside test used by anesthesiologists and other healthcare providers as part of the preoperative airway assessment. Its purpose is to predict the ease of direct laryngoscopy and tracheal intubation. The test involves assessing the visibility of specific anatomical structures in the pharynx. The patient is asked to sit upright, open their mouth as wide as possible, and protrude their tongue without phonating. The view is then graded from Class I (full visibility of the soft palate, fauces, uvula, and pillars) to Class IV (only the hard palate is visible). A higher Mallampati score (Class III or IV) correlates with a more restricted view of the glottis during laryngoscopy and a higher likelihood of encountering a difficult intubation. It is one of several tools used to anticipate and prepare for potential airway management challenges.",
