      "explanation": "The Bragg peak is a characteristic physical phenomenon describing the energy loss of heavy charged particles as they travel through matter. Unlike photons (X-rays) or electrons, which deposit their energy gradually from the surface inwards, a heavy charged particle like a proton travels through tissue and deposits a relatively small amount of energy along its initial path. As the particle slows down, its rate of energy loss increases dramatically, reaching a sharp maximum at a specific depth. This sharp peak in energy deposition is the Bragg peak. Beyond this peak, the energy deposition falls off to almost zero very rapidly. This physical property is exploited in proton beam therapy for cancer. It allows for the delivery of a very high, conformal dose of radiation precisely to the tumor volume, while sparing the healthy tissues located both in front of and, most importantly, behind the tumor.",
